description:
tests/contacts_tests.rs:228-232:

    let err_str = format!("{err:?}");
    assert!(
        err_str.contains("accountNotFound") || err_str.contains("AccountNotFound"),
        "must be accountNotFound: {err_str}"
    );

The test asserts on the Debug-format string of JmapError. The `format!("{err:?}").contains(...)` pattern is the same anti-pattern as JMAP-qz9v.18 (which already flagged the inline test in addressbook.rs), and adds an extra wrinkle: the OR matches "accountNotFound" OR "AccountNotFound" — i.e. the test deliberately accepts both wire-format spelling and Rust enum-variant capitalization, because the author was not sure which would appear in the Debug output.

Idiomatic Rust answer: pattern-match on err.error_type.as_str() the same way the inline tests in card.rs:541 and addressbook.rs:482 already do:

    assert_eq!(err.error_type.as_str(), "accountNotFound");

That's a typed assertion against the wire-format string defined by RFC 8620 §3.6.2; no Debug format string parsing, no OR fallback for capitalization variants.

This duplicates the idiom error from existing finding .18 in a different file. The fix is mechanical and the same.

Engineering cost: low (test does pass), but Debug format strings are explicitly NOT part of the API contract per workspace AGENTS.md — any future revision of JmapError's Debug derive silently breaks this test in a way that looks unrelated to error semantics. The OR fallback for capitalization tells you the author noticed the format was unstable and chose to paper over it.
